he hill.\n- tile_id 0x31 (49) = $131 = left top slope (col4,row21).\n- tile_id 0x32 (50) = $132 = right top slope (col5,row21).\n- tile_id 0x33 (51) = $133 = right edge (col6,row22; col7,row23; col8,row24; col9,row25). Right diagonal edge.\n- tile_id 0x26 (38) = $126 = interior fill (body). It's everywhere inside.\n- tile_id 0x34 (52) = $134 = the dark \"eye\" spots inside the hill (col5,row22; col3,row24; col6,row24). In the screenshot the hill has two dark marks (like eyes). Actually there are 3 dark marks: at row22 col5, row24 col3, row24 col6. Looking at the screenshot, the hill has two dark dots. Hmm, the hill in SMB has two dark \"eye\" spots. Actually the hill object in SMB 1-1 has 2 dark spots. Let me look at the picture again: the hill shows two black marks. Hmm.\n\nWait, the nametable read is from the live nametable; the visible hill may be partially off-screen on the left (col0 is at x=0). Actually the hill's full width is 10 tiles = 80px, from col0 to col9, i.e. x=0..79. But the hill object in SMB is 5 tiles wide... Hmm, actually the hill in SMB 1-1 is composed as: a wide base with two slopes.\n\nHold on, this nametable region is 12 cols x 6 rows starting at col0. But is this the hill or is the hill mirrored? Looking at the screenshot, the hill's peak is around x=40 (=col5). Left slope descends to x=0 at row 25. Right slope descends to x=80 at row 25. So the hill spans x=0..80. Yes, that matches: a big hill 10 tiles wide, 5 tiles tall.\n\nHmm, but actually the classic SMB1 hill is 5x3 tiles or so. This looks like a \"big hill\" variant. Whatever — the data is what it is.\n\nActually wait. Looking at the screenshot more carefully: the hill appears to span roughly x=8..85 with peak around x=45, and its base at y≈207. The base row 25 = y 200-207. Yes.\n\nHmm but the hill left edge goes to x=0 (col0 at row25). In the screenshot, at the far left bottom, the hill's left slope reaches x=0? The brick ground starts at y=208 (row 26). And left of the hill, at x=0.